# THE INVARIANT: this instance's TRACKED tree stays identical to the template's.
#
# That is what makes `git pull template main` a clean fast-forward every time
# and never a merge. Nothing a running fleet writes is tracked, so there is no
# divergence, so there is nothing to conflict. The gitignore split and
# scripts/update-from-template.sh are one design, not two.
#
# Every entry below says WHY it is here, because the updater depends on this
# split and an entry nobody understands is one somebody eventually removes.
# Three reasons, and no others:
#
#   GENERATED   written by a script from a live source; reproducible
#   LOG         the record of something that happened in THIS instance
#   PER-INSTANCE  true on one machine, or authored by one operator
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS, stated plainly because the README says it too: this repo no
# longer backs up your control plane's content. It distributes the template's
# SHAPE. Your run logs, your project context and your map live in one working
# copy and die with it unless you back that copy up yourself.
#
# Files already tracked in an older instance are unaffected — git applies these
# rules to untracked paths only. update-from-template.sh reports any it finds.

# PER-INSTANCE. Rendered from extension.toml.in by scripts/install-extension.sh
# and carrying this clone's absolute path, which is true only on one machine —
# extension.toml.in's header says why the path cannot be a `~`.
/extension.toml

# PER-INSTANCE. Your GitHub owners. registry/owners.example.txt is the tracked
# copy that documents the format.
/registry/owners.txt

# GENERATED. Rebuilt wholesale from your `gh` session by scripts/sync-registry.sh.
/registry/repos.generated.yaml

# PER-INSTANCE. The judgement about one of your projects, written from
# _TEMPLATE.md. The template ships the form; what you write into it is yours.
/registry/context/*.md
!/registry/context/_TEMPLATE.md

# LOG. One file per orchestration run — the record of what this instance did.
# _TEMPLATE.md is the shipped form and stays.
/orchestration/runs/*.md
!/orchestration/runs/_TEMPLATE.md

# PER-INSTANCE. Playbooks you write. They live in their own directory rather
# than beside the template's so this stays ONE rule that never needs a line
# adding when the template ships a fourth playbook — a forgotten negation would
# land that playbook ignored in every instance, silently.
/orchestration/playbooks/local/*
!/orchestration/playbooks/local/README.md

# PER-INSTANCE. Your session-profile overrides, layered over the tracked
# defaults in orchestration/session-profiles.yaml. scripts/session-flags.sh
# reads both; a profile defined here replaces the shipped one of that name.
/orchestration/session-profiles.local.yaml

# LOG + PER-INSTANCE. The task queue: topics, per-task briefs, the transitions
# a run produced, and what each worker concluded. This is the control plane's
# working state — one operator's prompts and one operator's work — and it is
# exactly what must not be tracked, or every instance's queue would collide on
# the next pull. README.md is the shipped explanation of the layout and stays,
# the same shape /orchestration/playbooks/local/ uses.
/orchestration/queue/*
!/orchestration/queue/README.md

# GENERATED. Python's bytecode cache. scripts/lib/webui.py loads queue.py as a
# module, and importing anything writes one of these beside the source — so it
# appears the first time the monitor is started and never in a fresh clone.
__pycache__/

# LOG + PER-INSTANCE. The monitor's runtime state: the port it chose at bind
# time, its supervisor's pid, its log, and the `down` flag that keeps an
# explicit stop honoured across a restart, a reboot and the next onboarding
# run. The SERVER'S CODE is template-owned and tracked (scripts/webui.sh,
# scripts/lib/webui.py); everything it writes is this instance's and is not.
/orchestration/webui/
